      <span>•</span>
      <span>NCMA Design Standards</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      When Does a Retaining Wall Need Geogrid Reinforcement?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Segmental retaining wall blocks rely on their own weight and rear shear lips to hold back soil up to roughly 36 to 48 inches. Beyond that height, the self-weight of the blocks is insufficient to prevent catastrophic overturning. Here is when, where, and how to install geogrid soil reinforcement per NCMA standards.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">The 3-Foot Rule</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Mandatory Above 36"–48"</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Embedment Length</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">Min 70% of Wall Height</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Vertical Course Spacing</div>
