    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">What is Geogrid and How Does It Work?</h2>
    <p>
      Geogrid is a high-tensile polyester or polypropylene mesh coated with PVC. When laid horizontally between courses of retaining blocks and buried deep into the compacted backfill soil, geogrid locks with the gravel aggregate. Instead of the blocks holding back the hill, the geogrid turns the entire backfill soil zone into a monolithic, reinforced composite block (known as a <strong>Mechanically Stabilized Earth / MSE wall</strong>).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">When is Geogrid Legally Required?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Walls Under 36 Inches (3 Feet):</strong> Typically do not require geogrid, provided the ground above the wall is flat and native soil is well-drained.</li>
      <li><strong>Walls Between 36 and 48 Inches:</strong> Highly recommended in clay soils, freeze-thaw zones, or anywhere heavy rainfall occurs.</li>
      <li><strong>Walls Over 48 Inches (4 Feet):</strong> Strictly required by the International Building Code (IBC Section 1807.2) and municipal permit offices. A stamped structural engineer design is usually legally required above 4 feet.</li>
      <li><strong>Slopes or Driveways Above the Wall (Surcharge Loads):</strong> If a steep hillside slopes down toward the top of the wall, or if cars park within a distance equal to the wall height, geogrid is mandatory even on small 2.5-foot walls.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Engineering Calculation: How Long Must Geogrid Be?</h2>
    <p>
      The National Concrete Masonry Association (NCMA) formula for minimum geogrid embedment length ($L$) is:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 font-bold mb-2">Embedment Length = Total Wall Height &times; 0.70 (Minimum 4.0 Feet)</div>
      <ul class="text-xs text-gray-600 space-y-1 mb-0 list-disc pl-4 mt-2">
        <li><strong>4-Foot Wall:</strong> 4 ft &times; 0.70 = 2.8 ft &rarr; Minimum code requires <strong>4.0 feet</strong> embedment length.</li>
        <li><strong>6-Foot Wall:</strong> 6 ft &times; 0.70 = <strong>4.2 feet</strong> minimum embedment length.</li>
        <li><strong>8-Foot Wall:</strong> 8 ft &times; 0.70 = <strong>5.6 feet</strong> minimum embedment length.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Directional Geogrid Orientation Matters (Uni-Axial vs Bi-Axial)"
    note="Geogrid comes in two distinct manufacturing weaves: Bi-axial (equal strength in both directions) and Uni-axial (high-strength in one direction only). Most commercial retaining wall geogrid (like Miragrid or Stratagrid) is uni-axial. The high-tensile ribs run along the length of the roll. That means the roll must be rolled out perpendicular to the wall face (from front to back), NEVER unrolled parallel along the wall length! If you lay uni-axial geogrid sideways along the wall, it has near-zero tensile strength and the wall will fail during the first heavy rain."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Geogrid Installation"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Overlapping Adjacent Geogrid Sheets on the Same Course",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (Wobbly Blocks & Lost Interlock)",
        description: "Overlapping geogrid layers directly between block joints creates a double-thickness fabric bump that tilts blocks forward or breaks rear shear pins.",
        solution: "Place adjacent geogrid sheets tight edge-to-edge with 100% continuous coverage without overlapping between the concrete block courses."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Leaving Slack in the Geogrid During Backfilling",
        costImpact: "$2,400 (Delayed Wall Movement & Settlement)",
        description: "If geogrid has ripples or slack when gravel is dumped on it, the wall must shift forward 2 inches before the grid engages and becomes taut.",
        solution: "Pull the geogrid completely taut toward the back of the excavation trench and stake it into the dirt with landscape staples before dumping aggregate."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Running Heavy Compaction Equipment Directly on Bare Grid",
        costImpact: "$1,200 (Torn Polymer Ribs)",
        description: "Driving tracked skid steers or heavy steel drum rollers directly over bare geogrid punctures and shreds the polymer mesh.",
        solution: "Spread a minimum 6-inch layer of crushed aggregate over the geogrid before operating mechanical equipment or heavy rollers."
      }
    ]}
  />

